# P1S direct-MQTT LED controller

Firmware for an ESP32-C3 that drives an 81-pixel addressable LED strip from
read-only Bambu P1S local MQTT telemetry.

## Build and flash

1. Install [PlatformIO](https://platformio.org/) in VS Code.
2. Open this directory and select the `esp32-c3-devkitm-1` environment. Change
   `board` in `platformio.ini` if your custom board needs another ESP32-C3
   definition.
3. Build, upload, then open the serial monitor at 115200 baud.
4. On first start (or if Wi-Fi cannot be joined), the controller opens a setup
   Wi-Fi network named `P1S-LED-Setup-XXXX`. Its password is printed only on
   the serial monitor. Browse to `http://192.168.4.1` and save your settings.

The dashboard intentionally never returns the LAN access code or Wi-Fi
password. It has no authentication, so use it only on a trusted local network.
Use a DHCP reservation for the printer so its IP address remains stable.

## P1S configuration

Enable the local/LAN/developer mode supported by the installed P1S firmware,
then copy the printer's IP address, serial number, and LAN access code into the
setup page. This program connects only to:

`device/<serial>/report`

It never publishes to the printer request topic and cannot control the printer.

P1S local MQTT uses TLS on port 8883. Export the printer broker's PEM CA/server
certificate and paste it into the dashboard. TLS is deliberately not bypassed:
the controller refuses to connect until a PEM certificate is supplied.

## Hardware

- LED data: GPIO4, 81 pixels (set `LED_COUNT` in `src/main.cpp` if needed)
- I2S microphone (diagnostic amplitude only): WS GPIO6, SCK GPIO5, SD GPIO7

The strip must share ground with the ESP32 and have sufficient 5 V current.
The 5 V USB port must be able to supply the strip's worst-case draw; brightness
defaults to a conservative 96/255. A 3.3 V-to-5 V level shifter is recommended
for a 5 V LED data input.
